  1.202.5+2.12-1ubuntu7.3 (noble-updates), unchanged (source: https://packages.ubuntu.com/source/noble/grub2 and https://git.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/grub2). UEFI Secure Boot
  accepts it because Canonical signed it; the shim that starts it comes from the Ubuntu ISO that is being booted. Used only for Ubuntu started with Secure Boot on.
- wimlib (https://wimlib.net), LGPL-3.0-or-later: NOT shipped; the server runs the wimlib-imagex that is installed on the computer (Debian/Ubuntu: package wimtools) to put two files into a copy of a Windows boot.wim.
- wimboot 2.9.0 (https://ipxe.org/wimboot), GPL-2.0-or-later, source at https://github.com/ipxe/wimboot. SHIPPED unchanged in internal/assets/ipxe/wimboot (downloaded 2026-10-21
  from the project's release, SHA-256 5f067ccdc4d084d5bf77b6c853bd0f8402dfc2b4cd1b103d358993ae97fae8e3). Starts Windows setup images.
- go-smb-server (https://github.com/sonroyaalmerol/go-smb-server), MIT, Copyright (c) its authors. SHIPPED in third_party/go-smb-server with our changes: the SMB 2
  server that shows an ISO to Windows' set-up environment. Changes (so that Windows 11 can use it): a security blob in the negotiate answer and real times and sizes,
  the NTLM challenge with a full target information and a version, more file and file system information classes, the directory listing classes Windows asks for
  with listings that continue over several requests, shorter minimum request sizes, and the volume and file system answers laid out as Windows reads them, as many credits as the client asks for (one per answer
  made Windows' set-up environment stop at its first folder listing), no oplocks, and no "no caching" share flag.
- Samba (https://www.samba.org), GPL-3.0-or-later. Not used: the built-in share above replaces it.
- 7-Zip (https://www.7-zip.org), LGPL and BSD parts. Planned: listing ISO contents, run as a separate program.

